You are part of a C++-to-Rust migration task. Your specific assignment: PROBE: Characterize the exact behavior of the reference C++ binary /workspace/dataset/test14_executable. Run it with a matrix of inputs and record exact stdout bytes (use od -c or xxd) and exit codes: no arguments; n = 0,1,2,3,4,5,14,20,100; a negative n (e.g. -1, -5); a non-numeric arg (e.g. abc); an arg with trailing junk (e.g. '7xyz', '  3', '+6'); multiple args (extra args ignored?); a large n like 1000000 (time it briefly). Confirm precisely: the trailing whitespace/newline layout of each line, the n=0 behavior (is there a trailing empty line?), behavior for negative n (does it crash, hang, print something?), and what happens on stoi parse failure (exception? abort? exit code?). Write your findings as a compact behavior spec table. Do NOT create or modify anything in /output — you are read-only probing; use /tmp for any scratch files.

Shared context:
- The C++ source is at /workspace/dataset/immer/tests/test14.cpp (also reproduced below). It uses the immer persistent vector library: builds an immer::vector<int> of 0..n-1, converts to a transient, does set(n/2, n*10), push_back(n*20), push_back(n*30), converts back to persistent, then prints: "v.size() v2.size()", "v2[n/2]", and the last 3 elements of v2 followed by a newline. n comes from argv[1] via std::stoi, default 14.
- Reference compiled C++ binary: /workspace/dataset/test14_executable
- Toolchain: rustc 1.75.0 and cargo at /usr/bin. Edition 2021. NO external crates — std only.
- Known outputs: n=0 → "0 2\n0\n\n" (the last-3 loop does not run because of size_t wraparound semantics: v2.size()-3 wraps and the int loop variable converted back to size_t makes the condition false); n=1 → "1 3\n10\n10 20 30\n"; n=2 → "2 4\n20\n20 40 60\n"; n=3 → "3 5\n30\n2 60 90\n"; n=4 → "4 6\n40\n3 80 120\n".

C++ source for reference:
```cpp
#include <immer/vector.hpp>
#include <immer/vector_transient.hpp>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
    int n = (argc > 1) ? std::stoi(argv[1]) : 14;
    auto v = immer::vector<int>{};
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) { v = v.push_back(i); }
    auto t = v.transient();
    t.set(n/2, n * 10);
    t.push_back(n * 20);
    t.push_back(n * 30);
    auto v2 = t.persistent();
    std::cout << v.size() << " " << v2.size() << std::endl;
    std::cout << v2[n/2] << std::endl;
    for (int i = v2.size() - 3; i < v2.size(); ++i) { std::cout << v2[i] << " "; }
    std::cout << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

Report back concisely: what you did, exact commands run, and the results (file paths created, compile status, byte-level output comparisons).

